<comment datatype='string'>This enzyme has not been purified from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-43687&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Metallosphaera sedula&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; in the initial study of the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-5789&quot;&gt;3-hydroxypropanoate/4-hydroxybutanate cycle&lt;/a&gt; enzymes. Although the activity was measured in cell-free extracts, the identity of the gene encoding it was not known at the time [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18079405&quot;&gt;Berg07&lt;/a&gt;]. 
&lt;p&gt;
In a later study, a search of the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-43687&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Metallosphaera sedula&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; genome revealed a gene (&lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15031&quot;&gt;Msed_0656&lt;/a&gt;) that was highly similar to eubacterial &amp;beta;-ketothiolase enzymes [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21169482&quot;&gt;RamosVera11&lt;/a&gt;]. While the enzyme from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-43687&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Metallosphaera sedula&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; was not purified at the time, the homologous gene from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-444157&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Pyrobaculum neutrophilum&lt;/i&gt; V24Sta&lt;/a&gt; (&lt;i&gt;Tneu_0249&lt;/i&gt;) was cloned and expressed as a soluble, N-terminal His&lt;sub&gt;10&lt;/sub&gt;-tagged protein in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. The purified recombinant enzyme catalyzed the expected thiolytic cleavage of acetoacetyl-CoA into two molecules of acetyl-CoA [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21169482&quot;&gt;RamosVera11&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The gene from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-43687&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Metallosphaera sedula&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; was later cloned with an N-terminal His&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt; tag and produced recombinantly in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, along with the &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-10489&quot;&gt;hbs&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-10490&quot;&gt;hbd&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15030&quot;&gt;Msed_0399&lt;/a&gt; genes, which together catalyze the 4 last steps of the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-5789&quot;&gt;3-hydroxypropanoate/4-hydroxybutanate cycle&lt;/a&gt;. The predicted combined action of the four enzymes is the conversion of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=4-HYDROXY-BUTYRATE&quot;&gt;4-hydroxybutanoate&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETYL-COA&quot;&gt;acetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;. Indeed, when the four recombinant proteins were combined &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; they were able to catalyze this conversion [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/24532060&quot;&gt;Hawkins14&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
